---
import FeaturesLayout from '../../layouts/FeaturesLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<FeaturesLayout
  currentSlug="live-example"
  title="Live Example Tab - Hosted component demos in compodocx"
  description="Live demo iframes next to your API docs via <example-url> tags. Point at a demo you already host. exampleUrlBase resolves relative URLs against your demo app."
>
  <p class="text-cdx-text-secondary text-lg">
    Pages can surface a dedicated <em>Example</em> tab with one or more live demos. Point at any URL you
    control (a fixture page, a hosted demo, a StackBlitz project) and compodocx renders it inside an iframe
    alongside the symbol&rsquo;s API and source.
  </p>

  <h2>The marker</h2>
  <p>
    Use an HTML-style <code class="cdx-code">&lt;example-url&gt;</code> marker inside the JSDoc of the
    class it belongs to. Multiple markers render as multiple iframes inside one Example tab. The tab is
    hidden automatically when no marker is present, and a marker counts only for the class whose comment
    holds it.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    code={`/**
 * A selectable card surface.
 *
 * <example-url>https://demos.example.com/cards/article</example-url>
 * <example-url>https://demos.example.com/cards/action</example-url>
 */
@Component({ ... })
export class CardComponent { ... }`}
  />

  <p>
    Each URL renders as <code class="cdx-code">&lt;iframe class="cdx-example-container"&gt;</code>.
    Browsers that don&rsquo;t allow iframes show the localized
    <code class="cdx-code">no-iframes</code> fallback message.
  </p>

  <h2>Relative URLs</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code class="cdx-code">exampleUrlBase</code> (or <code class="cdx-code"
      >--exampleUrlBase</code
    >) and a relative marker such as <code class="cdx-code">/#/forms/select</code> is appended to it,
    so the tab loads from your demo app instead of the docs folder. Absolute URLs stay as written. Without
    a base, the build lists relative markers in one warning.
  </p>

  <h2>
    Example URLs vs. <code class="cdx-code">@example</code>
  </h2>
  <p>
    Both can coexist on the same component. <code class="cdx-code">@example</code> blocks render as syntax-highlighted
    code under an <em>Examples</em> section on the Info tab.
    <code class="cdx-code">&lt;example-url&gt;</code> renders runnable demos in a separate Example tab.
    Use <code class="cdx-code">@example</code> for snippets readers should copy,
    <code class="cdx-code">&lt;example-url&gt;</code> for behavior they want to see.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosting the demo</h2>
  <p>
    The marker accepts any URL: your own demo site, a CodeSandbox, a StackBlitz project, or a static
    fixture deployed alongside the docs. compodocx does not bundle a runtime sandbox; it links to
    whatever you already host.
  </p>
</FeaturesLayout>
